{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission-review3.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn 16 reviewed the corrected artifact, which the trusted runner reproduced with the whole recovery path driven.\\n\\nState on entry. The corrected check (artifact 8c65f177581c4812a6116f4a37b2b38f, executed as 23cff5113484421c9d76595f272184e1) reported violated with the oracle correspondence unreviewed. The retained stream shows the sequence recovery_seed, peer_committed_reply (COMMITTED, PUT), recovery_resolution with adopted_committed false, the drive of the restart's PreAccept round, accept_round_after_commit with accept_oks 0 to 1, and recovery_outcome with vectors_match false, accept_oks 1.\\n\\nReview judgement (checker_correspondence, no_issue_found). The first property encodes the written obligation (a slow-quorum COMMITTED reply must be adopted) and was violated; the second encodes the agreement form of the same requirement on the accept path and was also violated: the recoverer ends COMMITTED with a NOOP command while the replica that already committed the same instance holds a PUT. I checked each correspondence aspect: the entry and schedule now drive every queued round in order (Prepare/PrepareReply, the phase-1 restart's PreAccept and reply, then the Accept and its reply) and re-read InstanceSpace after each delivery because startPhase1 replaces the record object, which is exactly what fixed the earlier artefact where accept count and status were read from the wrong round; identity correlates prerequisite and outcome through the instance rather than through the compared values; the observation completes after the rounds and the trailing diagnostic shows the Commit the recoverer broadcast; the prepared COMMITTED record and the substituted trigger are declared in the claim scope. Alternatives and reservations are stated in the rationale and limits instead of hidden: the outcome property is conditional and would hold vacuously if the recoverer never committed (the committed flag is printed so applicability is visible), the propagation of the recoverer's Commit to the other replicas is not driven, and the alternative reading about a fresh ballot outranking an older committed vbal is answered for this path by the fact that two different committed commands for one instance is itself the disagreement.\\n\\nEffect. If the review is accepted, the consequence of the confirmed adoption defect is confirmed within the driven path: not a blocked catch-up but two competing committed vectors for one instance, an agreement concern rather than a progress one. The wider effects (other replicas' handling of the recoverer's Commit, client-visible divergence) stay explicitly out of scope, and no map object, requirement, premise or earlier observation was changed.\"}"}}
